# Start the keystore2 service.
#
# Device-tree override of bootable/recovery/etc/init/keystore2.rc.
#
# Why override at all: the stock file starts keystore2 from `on late-init`, which
# is independent of the vendor keymaster HAL that this device has to bring up from
# the ramdisk.  keystore2 resolves its TRUSTED_ENVIRONMENT SecurityLevel eagerly at
# startup (system/security/keystore2/src/service.rs:65-79) and that resolution is a
# ONE-SHOT binder lookup on this build:
#
#   globals.rs:344 get_keymint_device()
#     -> globals.rs:230 connect_keymint()
#        -> utils.rs:665 retry_get_interface(): retry_count = 1 unless cfg!(early_vm),
#           so the retry loop never runs and binder::get_interface() fails
#           immediately with NAME_NOT_FOUND when the HAL is not registered yet.
#
# A keystore2 that starts first therefore exits during startup and is restarted
# every 5 s (system/core/init/service.h:236), which is what gets the second
# attempt past the race.  Meanwhile vold's own barrier is only for keystore2:
# system/vold/Keystore.cpp:112-122 polls
# "android.system.keystore2.IKeystoreService/default" 300 x 100 ms (30 s) and only
# then asks for the security level, so a missing keystore2 is retried but a
# keystore2 that lost its keymaster connection is not.
#
# The first exit is expected by construction and must therefore not escalate:
# init reboots to the fatal target once crash_count_ exceeds 4
# (system/core/init/service.cpp:366 -> :383), and the window that normally bounds
# that count is disabled in recovery, because the guard is
#   if (now < time_crashed_ + fatal_crash_window_ || !boot_completed)
# (system/core/init/service.cpp:365) and a recovery ramdisk never sets
# sys.boot_completed.  In recovery the count is therefore unbounded and
# `critical` degenerates into "five failures at any point => reboot".  The
# on early-init block below disarms that escalation; see its own comment.
#
# Only the trigger changed; the service definition is byte-for-byte the platform
# one, including `critical window=0` and the recovery SELinux label.  Ordering is
# still a device-tree responsibility here: init.svc.keymaster-4-0 = running is set
# by init itself when that service is forked (system/core/init/service.cpp:173-192,
# called at :783/:884), and the HAL is only started after the QSEE daemon is up and
# has registered its listeners (see init.recovery.qcom.rc).  The process state is
# not the same as hwbinder registration; the README records that residual gap
# together with the platform-side wait that would close it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disarm the fatal-reboot escalation for this service.
#
# The lookup described above is one-shot, so the very first keystore2 start loses
# the race by construction: init sets init.svc.keymaster-4-0=running the moment it
# forks the HAL (system/core/init/service.cpp:173-192, called from :783/:884),
# while the HAL still has to load its trustlet through QSEECom before it can
# register.  One exit is the expected outcome and the 5 s restart is the fix for
# it -- but only if init is allowed to restart instead of rebooting.
#
# init.svc_debug.no_fatal.<service-name> is the documented switch for exactly
# this (system/core/init/README.md:242-243, tested at service.cpp:372): with it
# set, init keeps restarting keystore2 rather than aborting to the fatal target.
# It is set from early-init so it is already true long before the first start,
# which is triggered from keymaster's fork, i.e. from `on fs` at the earliest.
# vold's own 30 s barrier (system/vold/Keystore.cpp:112-122) still bounds how long
# TWRP waits for the service, so this cannot turn a dead keystore2 into a silent
# hang -- it only removes the reboot.
on early-init
    setprop init.svc_debug.no_fatal.keystore2 true

on property:init.svc.keymaster-4-0=running
    start keystore2

service keystore2 /system/bin/keystore2 /tmp/misc/keystore
    class early_hal
    user root
    group keystore readproc log
    task_profiles ProcessCapacityHigh
    # The default memlock limit of 65536 bytes is too low for keystore.
    rlimit memlock unlimited unlimited
    # Reboot to bootloader if Keystore crashes more than 4 times before `sys.boot_completed`.
    critical window=0
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0
